run out. She moved upstate with her mother, who cleaned homes to support them both, as they tried to carve out a place for themselves in the small rural town of Mexico, New York. It's not an kind of open, easy-going place. Andrea Geyer went up there to talk to people about Audrey. Nobody was unfriendly to me, but there's something about these close-knit communities where as a stranger, you don't feel welcomed. Audrey could not get used to small-town life.
见底了。她和母亲一起搬到了纽约州北部，母亲靠清洗房屋来养活两人，她们试图在纽约州墨西哥镇的一个小乡村里为自己开辟一方天地。那并不是一个开放、随和的地方。安德烈·盖尔去那里找人谈论奥黛丽的事。没有人对我不友好，但这种紧密的社区有一种特质，作为局外人，你不会感到受欢迎。奥黛丽无法习惯小镇生活。

27
00:11:39.000 --> 00:12:11.000
Her whole adulthood, she had been traveling around the country, studying art, working with artists, engaging in intellectual discussions, and wearing fine clothes. And suddenly she lost it all. Well, she still had the clothes. People told me that she was very flamboyant. She liked to dress up in colorful garments. I mean, I'm sure she had a pretty impressive wardrobe that was very outlandish for a small community. But it wasn't just Audrey's style that set her apart.
在她整个成年时期，她一直在全国各地旅行、研究艺术、与艺术家合作、参与知识分子讨论并穿着华丽的衣服。而突然之间，她失去了这一切。好吧，她至少还留着那些衣服。人们告诉我她非常张扬。她喜欢穿色彩鲜艳的衣服。我的意思是，我确信她有一个非常令人印象深刻的衣橱，对于一个小社区来说这实在是太奇特了。但不仅仅是奥黛丽的穿衣风格让她显得与众不同。

28
00:12:11.000 --> 00:12:36.000
She would dress up on that and then part of her workout routine in the city was roller skating. So, you know, you can imagine a beautiful woman with long hair and a turban on her head, you know, trying to roller skate on a country, unpaid country road that was quite a scene. The town knew Audrey as that crazy woman who used to get naked for money. Parents would close their windows whenever she came roller skating by.
她会盛装打扮，而在城市时的锻炼日常之一就是轮滑。所以，你可以想象一个留着长头发布置着头巾的美丽女人，试图在乡村坑洼不平的乡村公路上溜旱冰——那真是一场奇景。镇上的人都知道奥黛丽就是那个曾经为了钱而脱光衣服的疯女人。每当她溜冰路过时，家长们就会关上窗户。

29
00:12:36.000 --> 00:13:18.000
The kids of course were totally fascinated with her, but there was a general consensus that she was crazy, just because she was different. So it's not hard to imagine why she would have fallen into a depression. And on May 27, 1922, Audrey Munson attempted suicide by swallowing poison. I don't know how committed her suicide attempt was. Maybe it was just an expression of being somewhere at the end of her line. After her failed suicide attempt, several years went by, and Audrey's mother was struggling to provide for her depressed daughter, and she just couldn't do it anymore.
孩子们当然对她着迷不已，但大家的普遍共识是她疯了，仅仅因为她与众不同。所以不难想象她为什么会陷入抑郁。1922 年 5 月 27 日，奥黛丽·蒙森吞服毒药试图自杀。我不知道她当时的自杀决心有多坚定。也许这只是她走投无路时的一种情绪宣泄。在她自杀未遂后，几年过去了，奥黛丽的母亲一直在艰难地照顾抑郁的女儿，而她终于撑不下去了。

30
00:13:18.000 --> 00:13:51.000
On Audrey Munson's 40th birthday, June 8, 1931, her mother checked her into the state mental institution. At that time, it was extremely common for families to put relatives into these institutions in moments of financial hardship where they felt they couldn't care for a person. Audrey remained in the institution into her 90s. Then she was put in a nursing home about 30 miles up the road. But this home for the elderly was situated on this little forle in highway. And on the other side of the highway
在奥黛丽·蒙森 40 岁生日那天，即 1931 年 6 月 8 日，她的母亲将她送进了州立精神病院。在当时，在遭遇经济困难、觉得无法照顾某人时，家属将亲戚送到这些机构是非常普遍的做法。奥黛丽在精神病院一直住到了 90 多岁。后来，她被转移到路程向上三十英里外的一家疗养院。但这家老年公寓正好坐落在高速公路的一个小岔道上。而在高速公路的另一边

31
00:13:51.000 --> 00:14:24.000
was a little strip mall, which had a bar. And she was known to sneak out of the home for the elderly and sneak across the forle in highway to spend her evenings at the bar, ordering drinks and telling stories of her times as a model and as an actress. They could not stop this 90-something elegant older woman from running across four lanes of traffic to go to the bar. It's sadly meant that they put her back into the mental institution where she spent the
是一个带有酒吧的小型商业街。众所周知，她会偷偷溜出老年公寓，溜过四车道的高速公路，去酒吧度过她的夜晚，点一杯饮料，讲述她当年作为模特和演员的故事。大家无法阻止这个 90 多岁优雅的老太太横穿四车道交通去酒吧。这不幸地导致她被重新送回了精神病院，在那里她度过了

32
00:14:24.000 --> 00:14:54.000
rest of her life. Audrey Munson lived just short of her 105th birthday. She died in 1996. This public body that once represented truth, civic fame, memory, the universe, and the stars was hidden away for nearly two-thirds of her life. But that one-third, that glorious third, immortalized her, and placed her all over American cities, perched high, quietly, out of sight, staring down at us.
余下的生命。奥黛丽·蒙森在离 105 岁生日还有不久时去世。她于 1996 年离世。这个曾经代表着真理、公民荣誉、记忆、宇宙和群星的公众躯体，在她生命近三分之二的时间里都被隐藏了起来。但那三分之一，那辉煌的三分之一，使她永垂不朽，让她遍布美国城市，高高伫立，静静地、隐匿在视线之外，俯视着我们。

33
00:14:54.000 --> 00:15:46.000
I'm wondering if many of my readers have not stood before a masterpiece of lovely sculpture or remarkable painting of a young girl and ask themselves a question, where is she now, this model who was so beautiful? What has been her reward? Is she happy and prosperous? Or is she sad and far-lawn? Her beauty gone, leaving only memories in the wake. 99% Invisible was produced this week by Avery Trouffleman, with Katie Mangle, Sam Green's fan, Kurt Colstack, Delaney Hall, Shereefusef, and me Roman Mars. All Audrey Munson quotes and less otherwise noted were taken from a tell-all series called
